p.src:
When the spin is rotated, its state becomes $\alpha_i'=U_{ij}\alpha_j,
\qquad
\begin{pmatrix}\alpha_1'\\\alpha_2'\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
U_{11}&U_{12}\\
U_{21}&U_{22}
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}\alpha_1\\\alpha_2\end{pmatrix}.$ Thus spatial rotations possess a two-dimensional matrix representation acting on spinors. There is a global two-valuedness in this representation that we defer; locally, the matrices have the transformation properties needed below.

